      "id": 1,
      "text": "In 1902, the El Paso and Southwestern Railroad laid tracks nine miles east of the original mining camp, sparking the birth of New Hachita.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        8,
        15,
        16
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1",
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.legendsofamerica.com/nm-hachita/"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "The original settlement became known as Old Hachita, a ghost town that once mined silver until the 1920s.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        18
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.nativehistory.info/hachita-new-mexico-old-and-new/"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "The town’s name honors Saint Catherine of Siena, named by Suozzi after his mother’s patron saint.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        2,
        3
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1",
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.legendsofamerica.com/nm-hachita/"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "Even today, Hachita keeps a post office and a convenience store/gas station, showing life persists.",
